# Update Documentation on Code Changes

After making code changes, check whether the following files need updating:

## README.md

Update when you add, remove, or rename:

- Routes or API endpoints
- Environment variables or prerequisites
- Project structure (new files or directories)
- Dependencies or setup steps
- Available scripts in package.json
- SEO assets, analytics events, or auth wiring

## .env.example

Update when you add, remove, or rename:

- Environment variables used in `lib/env.ts` or `types/env.d.ts`
- Keep placeholder values descriptive
- Never put real secrets in `.env.example`

## Code Organization

- `app/` — Next.js App Router routes, layouts, `generateMetadata`, loading and error boundaries
- `components/ui/` — shadcn-style primitives only
- `components/layout/` — site-wide layout pieces such as header and footer
- `components/features/<domain>/` — feature-specific UI (for example `auth/`, `demo/`)
- `components/seo/` — JSON-LD and SEO helpers used in layouts
- `hooks/` — Client hooks only; prefix with `use`
- `lib/` — Shared utilities grouped by concern (`analytics/`, `auth/`, `errors/`, `query/`, `seo/`)
- `actions/` — Server Actions with Zod validation and `{ data, error }` results
- `types/` — Shared TypeScript types and ambient declarations
- `config/` — Site and SEO defaults (`site.ts`, `seo.ts`)

When adding a feature, prefer `components/features/<domain>/` and keep `components/ui/` for generic primitives only.

